@chapter Stack Backend
@node Stack Backend
@section Stack Backend

The stack backend refers to the virtual machine used to execute Fountain scripts in the Phantasia game engine.
This virtual machine is a 16-bit stack machine, similar to Forth, and is implemented in @file{Source/Code/7800/Routines/Forth.s}.

The machine operates on a parameter stack that holds 16-bit values. The stack grows downward in memory, and the stack pointer points to the next free slot (i.e., the address where the next item will be stored).

Below is a table of the opcodes (bytecodes) recognized by the stack backend, their mnemonic names, and their effect on the parameter stack.

@table @asis
@item ForthEOL (Done)
Not a traditional opcode; signals the end of the script. No stack effect.

@item ForthPushWord (PushWord)
Pushes a 16-bit value from the instruction stream onto the stack.
Stack effect: @dots{} @rightarrow @dots{}, @var{value}
Description: Reads two bytes from the instruction stream (little-endian) and pushes them as a 16-bit value.

@item ForthPushByte (PushByte)
Pushes an 8-bit value from the instruction stream, zero-extended to 16-bit, onto the stack.
Stack effect: @dots{} @rightarrow @dots{}, @var{value} (where @var{value} is in the range 0-255)
Description: Reads one byte from the instruction stream and pushes it as a 16-bit value with the high byte zero.

@item ForthSetByte (SetByte)
Stores the byte at the top of the stack to the memory address given by the next item on the stack.
Stack effect: @var{value}, @var{address} @rightarrow @dots{}
Description: Pops an address and a value (16-bit, but only the low byte is used). Stores the low byte of @var{value} at the 16-bit address @var{address}.

@item ForthGetByte (GetByte)
Fetches a byte from the memory address on top of the stack, zero-extends it to 16-bit, and pushes it.
Stack effect: @var{address} @rightarrow @dots{}, @var{value} (where @var{value} is in the range 0-255)
Description: Replaces the address on the stack with the zero-extended byte read from that address.

@item ForthSetWord (SetWord)
Stores the 16-bit value at the top of the stack to the memory address given by the next item on the stack.
Stack effect: @var{value}, @var{address} @rightarrow @dots{}
Description: Pops an address and a value. Stores the 16-bit @var{value} at the 16-bit address @var{address}.

@item ForthGetWord (GetWord)
Fetches a 16-bit value from the memory address on top of the stack and pushes it.
Stack effect: @var{address} @rightarrow @dots{}, @var{value}
Description: Replaces the address on the stack with the 16-bit value read from that address.

@item ForthDup (Dup)
Duplicates the top item on the stack.
Stack effect: @var{x} @rightarrow @dots{}, @var{x}, @var{x}
Description: Pushes a copy of the top item.

@item ForthDrop (Drop)
Discards the top item on the stack.
Stack effect: @var{x} @rightarrow @dots{}
Description: Removes the top item from the stack.

@item ForthExecute (Execute)
Calls the subroutine whose address is on top of the stack.
Stack effect: @var{address} @rightarrow @dots{}
Description: Pops the address and jumps to it. The stack is popped by two bytes (one 16-bit item) before the jump.

@item ForthUnless (Unless)
Conditional branch: if the top of stack is false (zero), skip the next two bytes in the instruction stream.
Stack effect: @var{condition} @rightarrow @dots{}
Description: Pops a 16-bit condition. If the condition is zero, the instruction pointer is advanced by two bytes (to skip a 16-bit offset). Otherwise, execution continues normally.

@item ForthWhen (When)
Conditional branch: if the top of stack is true (non-zero), continue; otherwise, skip the next two bytes in the instruction stream.
Stack effect: @var{condition} @rightarrow @dots{}
Description: Pops a 16-bit condition. If the condition is non-zero, execution continues normally. If the condition is zero, the instruction pointer is advanced by two bytes.

@item ForthGo (Go)
Unconditional jump: sets the instruction pointer to the address given by the next two bytes in the instruction stream.
Stack effect: @dots{} @rightarrow @dots{}
Description: Reads two bytes from the instruction stream (little-endian) and sets the instruction pointer to that address. Does not affect the stack.

@item ForthSwap (Swap)
Swaps the top two items on the stack.
Stack effect: @var{x}, @var{y} @rightarrow @dots{}, @var{y}, @var{x}
Description: Exchanges the top two items on the stack.
@end table

@subsection Stack Layout and Conventions

The parameter stack is implemented as a circular buffer in RAM, with the stack pointer (@code{ForthStack}) indicating the next free slot.
The stack stores 16-bit values in little-endian order (low byte at the lower address).

When the stack pointer is @var{SP}, the top of the stack is located at @code{StackMemory + @var{SP} + 2} and @code{StackMemory + @var{SP} + 3} for the low and high bytes, respectively.
The second item is at @code{StackMemory + @var{SP} + 4} and @code{StackMemory + @var{SP} + 5}.

The stack pointer is decremented by two for each push operation and incremented by two for each pop operation.

Stack underflow and overflow are checked by the interpreter; underflow results in a fault log, and overflow triggers a debug break.

@subsection Usage in Fountain Scripts

Fountain scripts are compiled to sequences of these opcodes by the Fountain compiler (@file{SkylineTool/src/fountain.lisp}).
The resulting bytecode is executed by the Forth interpreter during gameplay to drive cutscenes, dialogue, and other scripted events.

See the @file{Source/Code/7800/Routines/Forth.s} file for the full implementation of the interpreter and opcodes.
